<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bardas Phokas the Elder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 878–c. 968</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Bardas</a> Phokas the Elder was a Byzantine general of the first half of the 10th century, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Emperor Nikephoros</a> II Phokas and of the kouropalates Leo Phokas the Younger, and maternal grandfather of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a>. A son of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-phokas-the-elder">Nikephoros Phokas the Elder</a>, he fought at the disastrous Battle of Acheloos in 917, and in 941, as governor of the Theme of Armeniakon, held off the Rus' who had landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kourkouas">John Kourkouas</a> drove them out. Made supreme commander in the East in 945, he was repeatedly defeated by Sayf al-Daula, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and was replaced by his son Nikephoros in 954/955. As emperor, Nikephoros made him Caesar; he died about 968, aged 90.</p></div>
